WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.612
Coding Exercise 2 adds filtering.

00:00:02.612 --> 00:00:06.722
The result should contain only active patients who are fifty or older,

00:00:06.722 --> 00:00:09.637
and it should show the requested three columns.

00:00:09.637 --> 00:00:14.462
Before writing the WHERE clause, identify the two independent conditions.

00:00:14.462 --> 00:00:17.042
One condition is about the active flag.

00:00:17.042 --> 00:00:19.367
The other is about the age threshold.

00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:24.892
Then decide which logical operator should connect them if both requirements must be true.

00:00:24.892 --> 00:00:31.342
Run and Check, then inspect the youngest patient in the result to make sure the boundary is handled correctly.

00:00:31.342 --> 00:00:33.117
If too many rows appear, review the filter.

00:00:33.117 --> 00:00:40.257
If the correct rows appear in the wrong order, review ORDER BY rather than changing the WHERE condition.

00:00:40.257 --> 00:00:43.112
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:43.112 --> 00:00:48.862
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:48.862 --> 00:00:50.482
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:50.482 --> 00:00:55.102
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:55.102 --> 00:01:00.302
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:00.302 --> 00:01:06.667
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:08.952
not only whether the statement executes.
